Excellent observation! This is critical information that changes the diagnosis entirely.

The Real Root Cause

The problem is mobile-specific and relates to how your CSS handles .content-section.active on different viewports. On desktop, the sections are hidden correctly (except on the User Guide page, which is a clue). On mobile, they appear everywhere.

Looking at your mobile CSS (around lines 350-450), I found the issue:

Your mobile CSS is overriding the hiding mechanism!

Specifically, this section of your mobile CSS:

```css
@media (max-width: 768px) {
    /* Fix all rows to be full width */
    .main-content .row {
        display: flex !important;
        flex-direction: column !important;
        width: 100% !important;
        max-width: 100% !important;
        margin: 0 !important;
        padding: 0 !important;
    }
    
    /* All columns take full width */
    .main-content .row [class*="col-"],
    .main-content [class*="col-"] {
        width: 100% !important;
        max-width: 100% !important;
        flex: 0 0 100% !important;
        /* ... */
    }
}
```

These selectors are too broad and are forcing ALL .row elements to display, regardless of their parent section's active state. The CSS specificity battle is being lost on mobile.

Why the User Guide page shows them on desktop

The User Guide section is dynamically created by JavaScript and doesn't have the same structure. When you navigate to it, the CSS selector .content-section:not(#dashboard) .row.mt-4 doesn't apply because the User Guide section doesn't have that exact structure, but the mobile CSS forces all rows to display anyway.

